Tcl_ObjSetVar2: hold the new value across the variable lookup

When the lookup fails with TCL_LEAVE_ERR_MSG, TclObjVarErrMsg replaces the
interpreter result. If newValuePtr is that result (CatchObjCmdCallback
passes Tcl_GetObjResult(interp)), its last reference is released there, and
the following newValuePtr->refCount check reads freed memory. Reproducer:
Tcl_EvalEx(interp, "catch nosuchcmd nosuchns::v", -1, 0) under ASan.

Affects Tcl 8.6.16, core-8-6-branch (8.6.18) and main (9.x). Carried by
Hefesto until upstream ships a fix; see cmake/Tcl.cmake.

--- a/generic/tclVar.c
+++ b/generic/tclVar.c
@@ -1754,14 +1754,21 @@
 
     flags &= (TCL_GLOBAL_ONLY|TCL_NAMESPACE_ONLY|TCL_LEAVE_ERR_MSG
 	    |TCL_APPEND_VALUE|TCL_LIST_ELEMENT);
+
+    /*
+     * Hold newValuePtr across the lookup: when it fails with
+     * TCL_LEAVE_ERR_MSG, the error message replaces the interpreter result,
+     * which may be newValuePtr itself (catch passes Tcl_GetObjResult).
+     */
+
+    Tcl_IncrRefCount(newValuePtr);
     varPtr = TclObjLookupVarEx(interp, part1Ptr, part2Ptr, flags, "set",
 	    /*createPart1*/ 1, /*createPart2*/ 1, &arrayPtr);
     if (varPtr == NULL) {
-	if (newValuePtr->refCount == 0) {
-	    Tcl_DecrRefCount(newValuePtr);
-	}
+	Tcl_DecrRefCount(newValuePtr);	/* Frees it if nobody else holds it. */
 	return NULL;
     }
+    newValuePtr->refCount--;		/* Back to the caller's count. */
 
     return TclPtrSetVarIdx(interp, varPtr, arrayPtr, part1Ptr, part2Ptr,
 	    newValuePtr, flags, -1);
